00:05:27.729 --> 00:05:32.180
spirit in a black room that you slip messages in the door

00:05:32.199 --> 00:05:36.050
with a question and they will answer back to you, you never

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:39.949
see them because you are in the company. No, I mean the

00:05:39.969 --> 00:05:43.839
company is us, I mean myself and 100,000 other colleagues of

00:05:43.859 --> 00:05:46.970
mine, they are the company. If we are excited knowing what

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:50.829
we want to do, aligned, inspired, moving forward, learning,

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:53.930
attracting better people than we are all the time, the

00:05:53.949 --> 00:05:56.800
company is moving in that direction, it's progressing, it's

00:05:56.819 --> 00:05:59.740
growing. If we are all going different directions waiting

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:02.689
for somebody else to solve our problem not acting on things

00:06:02.709 --> 00:06:05.610
we should act like low performance or poor performance and

00:06:05.629 --> 00:06:09.629
feeling that we are big, everything is going to be fine, we

00:06:09.649 --> 00:06:12.730
will never fail, that's the first step into failing, and

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:16.759
that's doesn't get you to be a great company. So these two

00:06:16.779 --> 00:06:21.069
ideas now let's go into what we learned, three things that

00:06:21.089 --> 00:06:25.439
create a top performing culture. We are not saying this is

00:06:25.459 --> 00:06:29.209
the only way to do it, I am saying we have done it for 21

00:06:29.209 --> 00:06:32.209
years and has been worth. It's not perfect, we will never be

00:06:32.209 --> 00:06:35.870
but that has worth, okay. So the first three things are

00:06:35.889 --> 00:06:40.810
dream, people, culture. I will explain because the company

00:06:40.829 --> 00:06:45.810
is us, people, dream the idea of the dream is something that

00:06:45.829 --> 00:06:48.910
comes naturally, we always have dream in our lives, we have

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.349
always dreamt of something and that's what keeps us going.

00:06:51.349 --> 00:06:54.370
That Monday morning when you are tired, when it's snowy out

00:06:54.389 --> 00:07:01.779
there, when it's cold or too warm, you feel like oh man I

00:07:01.799 --> 00:07:05.379
have to bike to class but you have that dream, you have that

00:07:05.399 --> 00:07:10.439
dream of doing well at school, dating that person, being

00:07:10.459 --> 00:07:13.550
hired by that company, buying that car. So, dream is

00:07:13.569 --> 00:07:16.889
something that for human beings click I think much more than

00:07:16.909 --> 00:07:21.269
visions and missions. Missions are for military, visions are

00:07:21.289 --> 00:07:28.800
for, I don't know for whom but dream is something that we

00:07:28.819 --> 00:07:31.069
all understand because through all our life, dream is

00:07:31.069 --> 00:07:35.410
something that has always propped us up to next step. So in

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:38.180
our company we don't have vision, we don't have a mission,

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:41.930
we have dream, we want to be the best in what we do in a

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:44.430
better world that's it, okay. And why is dream important?

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.980
First because again it's about human beings, second because

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.939
dream big or dream small takes the same amount of energy,

00:07:50.959 --> 00:07:54.550
think about that, one or the other takes the same amount of

00:07:54.569 --> 00:07:58.329
energy, so why not dream big. If you don't do it for

00:07:58.349 --> 00:08:01.099
yourself, nobody will knock at your door and say hey your

00:08:01.099 --> 00:08:04.099
dreams are too small, why don't you aim here? Nobody will do

00:08:04.099 --> 00:08:06.599
that. In our own experience we have always reached our

00:08:06.599 --> 00:08:10.259
dreams, never the way we planned, that's real life because

00:08:10.279 --> 00:08:12.259
you learn, because competition does something, the

00:08:12.279 --> 00:08:14.909
environment changes. But if you have a dream that's

00:08:14.929 --> 00:08:18.790
stretched enough that you know 80% how to get there and the

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:21.399
other is 20, you learn along the way, you believe you can

00:08:21.399 --> 00:08:24.689
learn along the way, that's something that can inspire

00:08:24.709 --> 00:08:29.149
people, get the bar higher and getting the bar higher you

00:08:29.169 --> 00:08:32.200
tell the message, good is not good enough guys, if we want

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:35.970
to be the best in what we do and go from here to here, that'

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:39.470
s the gap, we need the very best to get us there. So the

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:42.720
dream is only good if people buy into it, if they commit to

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.720
it, if they bring their passion to it, that's why I don't

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.970
like the word fun, I think fun is too weak. I don't like

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:52.220
people in our company to have fun, I have fun at the beach

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:55.470
with my kids, I like people in the company to have fun sure,

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:58.090
plus passion, plus commitment, plus energy lots of other

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:04.500
things. Fun is too weak, you just want to be the best in

00:09:04.519 --> 00:09:07.519
what we do. But there are differences, again the dream is to

00:09:07.519 --> 00:09:10.899
be something that inspires and credible enough so people buy

00:09:10.919 --> 00:09:14.079
into it because you have to work hard, it's not a target,

00:09:14.079 --> 00:09:18.080
it's a dream, and by definition it's a tough one, it's a

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:20.850
stretched one. It's not an adventure. If you put out there

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:23.850
an adventure and say guys, we are going to double the amount

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:27.100
of the company next year, people say oh my god, okay you are

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:30.100
going to commit to that, I am going to do something else.

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:32.850
That's an adventure. An adventure is when you know 20% how

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:35.600
to get there and you believe, in our experience, that 80%

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.350
you can discover along the way. Dream is the opposite. You

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:41.589
know 80% how to get there but at 20% stretch as per our

00:09:41.589 --> 00:09:44.529
experience is what you learn. And the learning curve,

00:09:44.549 --> 00:09:47.240
copying from others, tripping over things that you didn't

00:09:47.259 --> 00:09:50.399
know were there or just learning from the environment, that'

00:09:50.419 --> 00:09:54.909
s the dream. And your dream has to be powerful enough to

00:09:54.929 --> 00:09:57.679
commit people, and again dream big or small takes the same

00:09:57.679 --> 00:10:01.779
amount of energy. The other thing about the dream that's

00:10:01.799 --> 00:10:05.889
very important is that dream brings the best of people,

00:10:05.909 --> 00:10:10.379
think about this. Think about a high jump. I mean dream is

00:10:10.399 --> 00:10:13.100
about this. I mean you put the threshold here, you put the

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:17.600
bar here; which human being having the bar here even if the

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:22.200
guy could would jump here because we are rational, we

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:26.350
optimize energy, we optimize time. So if the bar is here, we

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:29.620
are going to jump enough to clear the hurdle even if you can

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:33.409
jump here. So the dream is that is the high jump, is to keep

00:10:33.429 --> 00:10:36.689
putting the bar higher so you can see what you, the company,

00:10:36.709 --> 00:10:41.000
the team, the group can do, and as the leader, it's an art

00:10:41.019 --> 00:10:43.490
and science. You have to keep putting that bar higher and

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:46.879
higher until you find that limit with what you have today

00:10:46.899 --> 00:10:49.759
and then you learn some more and then next year you can do a

00:10:49.759 --> 00:10:54.779
bit higher what's the maximum performance you can deliver.

00:10:54.799 --> 00:10:57.179
So that's the first thing and that's very important because

00:10:57.179 --> 00:11:01.069
if you don't buy into the dream, a lot of things I am going

00:11:01.089 --> 00:11:04.339
to say from now on don't make any sense. If you are just

00:11:04.339 --> 00:11:07.839
trying to survive as a company 9 to 6 you know just have a

00:11:07.839 --> 00:11:10.589
job because I have other interests and that's it, forget it,

00:11:10.589 --> 00:11:13.339
that's not the company for you that kind of company, okay.

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:15.629
So that's why the dream is very important.

00:11:15.649 --> 00:11:18.159
Second thing, you can only have dream if you have people

00:11:18.159 --> 00:11:22.269
because people are the ones dreaming, okay. Couple of

00:11:22.289 --> 00:11:27.919
things, great companies are formed by great people, obvious

00:11:27.939 --> 00:11:30.649
but so many people forget about it, people think that great

00:11:30.649 --> 00:11:33.620
companies are formed by great products, great cash flow,

00:11:33.639 --> 00:11:37.439
great installations, assets, asset base, no. Even the

00:11:37.459 --> 00:11:42.399
consumer goods company what distinguishes you from an

00:11:42.419 --> 00:11:44.699
average company is the kind of people you can attract,

00:11:44.699 --> 00:11:48.939
retain, deploy, develop, train, promote within your company

00:11:48.959 --> 00:11:53.090
that's it because behind the brand that's doing well in the

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:56.100
market, you have people that understood consumers, had the

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:59.049
insights, execute according to the insights and translated

00:11:59.069 --> 00:12:02.370
all that behind a brand, that's why the brand is successful

00:12:02.389 --> 00:12:05.919
not because of the brand. The brand was there was the second

00:12:05.939 --> 00:12:08.689
step, first step was to have great people, okay. So again

00:12:08.689 --> 00:12:11.970
great companies can only be formed by great people. Second

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:16.279
thing, great people attract more great people, that's

00:12:16.299 --> 00:12:19.860
important because the opposite is very sad, mediocre people

00:12:19.879 --> 00:12:23.799
attract more mediocre people, they like to work together you

00:12:23.819 --> 00:12:31.730
know. It's amazing, mediocre people love to work together

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:35.329
because nobody challenges anybody, the targets that they

00:12:35.349 --> 00:12:39.579
give to each other are kind of easy, you know a 120% how to

00:12:39.599 --> 00:12:43.569
get there January 1st so it's piece of cake, everything is

00:12:43.589 --> 00:12:47.769
fine, the feedbacks and your unit performance evaluation is

00:12:47.789 --> 00:12:51.279
always like you are a great guy, no gaps, I mean you know

00:12:51.299 --> 00:12:54.299
you are terrific, you are going to be the next president of

00:12:54.299 --> 00:12:57.529
this company one day you are so great, mediocre people, they

00:12:57.549 --> 00:13:02.730
love that. Great people is the opposite. They love to

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:05.539
challenge each other, they don't take it personally because

00:13:05.559 --> 00:13:08.879
they know it's for the best of the business, they know it's

00:13:08.879 --> 00:13:12.439
not about themselves about the business. So they know there

00:13:12.459 --> 00:13:15.819
is no hidden agenda so it can challenge them because it's

00:13:15.839 --> 00:13:18.449
all about what's best for the business. And they like to

00:13:18.449 --> 00:13:20.980
work with each other because by challenging one another,

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.579
they keep raising the bar and that brings the best in you

00:13:23.579 --> 00:13:27.069
that forces you to continue to be updated and learn because

00:13:27.089 --> 00:13:30.509
everybody else is pressuring you. That's why it's so good to

00:13:30.529 --> 00:13:33.279
hire people that are better than you because they force you

00:13:33.279 --> 00:13:36.279
to be better. If you had a whole bunch of mediocre people

00:13:36.279 --> 00:13:39.279
that think that you are the greatest guy on earth, you are

00:13:39.279 --> 00:13:41.779
going to start going like this because you have no

00:13:41.779 --> 00:13:45.509
incentive, no push to get better, okay. That's the other

00:13:45.529 --> 00:13:46.769
thing.

00:13:46.789 --> 00:13:50.210
What's great? You say I am saying great people attract more

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:53.699
of great people, what's great? If there was a lack of

00:13:53.719 --> 00:13:56.960
definition anywhere, just take you and your colleagues as a

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:00.789
benchmark, let's see if you were great. But if this is what

00:14:00.809 --> 00:14:03.889
we know, myself and my colleagues, I want to hire people

00:14:03.909 --> 00:14:06.439
that with the right training and right development can be

00:14:06.459 --> 00:14:11.779
better in some years than we are today, that's great. And

00:14:11.799 --> 00:14:14.709
smart people are not afraid of that, mediocre people are

00:14:14.729 --> 00:14:17.929
very afraid of that. They don't want very bright people in

00:14:17.949 --> 00:14:21.169
their teams because that's going to overshadow me, that's

00:14:21.189 --> 00:14:25.579
going to put me out of job. Smart people, even for the wrong

00:14:25.599 --> 00:14:28.789
reasons they arrive at the same place. It's much better to

00:14:28.809 --> 00:14:32.840
have a team of high achievers because that will help you as

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:36.819
a leader to get targets in a more consistent way and will

00:14:36.839 --> 00:14:40.169
help you to get promoted faster because you have a pipeline

00:14:40.189 --> 00:14:43.669
of people. So even if you are selfish, so even if you are

00:14:43.689 --> 00:14:46.209
thinking about the wrong things, only think about you not

00:14:46.229 --> 00:14:49.229
about the business, you get to the same place. But it's

00:14:49.229 --> 00:14:52.720
better to have in your team great people, very talented

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:57.159
people because that will be good for you and if you think in

00:14:57.179 --> 00:15:00.340
a more noble way, that's the best thing for the company,

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:04.720
okay. So we look at leaders and one of the traits that we

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:08.360
look is what's the capacity that they have to form, find,

00:15:08.379 --> 00:15:12.009
recruit, retain, develop people because leaders that can

00:15:12.029 --> 00:15:15.250
only use people that were developed by others, they are good

00:15:15.269 --> 00:15:18.819
leaders but they are not the ones that will get you 20 years

00:15:18.839 --> 00:15:21.639
from now, 30 years from now with the kind of pipeline that

00:15:21.639 --> 00:15:25.759
you need, okay. So we are talking about great people. In our

00:15:25.779 --> 00:15:30.819
experience, what kind of environment keeps great people on

00:15:30.839 --> 00:15:33.750
board because great people are very volatile, they could be,

00:15:33.769 --> 00:15:36.840
especially here in Silicon Valley. I mean if you have

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:39.929
somebody who is very good, can be hired by somebody else two

00:15:39.949 --> 00:15:42.449
years here, one year here. Great people in our experience

00:15:42.449 --> 00:15:45.819
like three things; they like to work in a place where

00:15:45.839 --> 00:15:49.419
meritocracy is the name of the game, informality is the way

00:15:49.439 --> 00:15:54.199
things are done and where candor is the way to go. Okay

00:15:54.219 --> 00:15:58.959
meritocracy, easier said than done. Again in sports of

00:15:58.979 --> 00:16:02.689
course meritocracy, the good players play, the others don't;

00:16:02.709 --> 00:16:05.990
in school of course if you have an F you know exactly what

00:16:06.009 --> 00:16:09.259
it means, you fail, if you have an A you got the scholarship

00:16:09.259 --> 00:16:12.279
of course. But then you got to the company, performance

00:16:12.299 --> 00:16:16.740
evaluations, that's straightforward because you say well

00:16:16.759 --> 00:16:20.009
today is Friday poor guy, I mean poor me and poor him and

00:16:20.009 --> 00:16:23.259
poor her, I will wait for the Monday and then Monday you are

00:16:23.259 --> 00:16:26.559
traveling, goes by two years, 10 years and you never said to

00:16:26.579 --> 00:16:31.049
your co-worker what this person needs to listen, you play

00:16:31.069 --> 00:16:34.759
god, you play god. You say no, no I am going to manage it

00:16:34.779 --> 00:16:38.029
this way, I am not going to say today because I think by

00:16:38.029 --> 00:16:42.279
himself for some strange reasons he is going to click to it,

00:16:42.299 --> 00:16:46.279
get better and therefore I can avoid this moment that we are

00:16:46.299 --> 00:16:49.149
going to have now. Of course it never happens because this

00:16:49.169 --> 00:16:53.179
click it doesn't happen. Constructive feedback is what gets

00:16:53.199 --> 00:16:57.269
people to get better. In my life, I owe a lot to what I

00:16:57.289 --> 00:17:00.639
learned to constructive and respectful feedback. That's what

00:17:00.659 --> 00:17:03.459
gets you to progress, people that are interested in your

00:17:03.479 --> 00:17:06.799
success. But for that, you have to have tough conversations,

00:17:06.819 --> 00:17:10.819
it's not everything rosy, okay. So again they like

00:17:10.839 --> 00:17:13.779
meritocracy and think about this, it's so easy again peer

00:17:13.799 --> 00:17:18.500
pressure, you go to a company, you lead a business unit, you

00:17:18.519 --> 00:17:21.299
have a vacancy, somebody got promoted and you have to fill

00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:25.839
that vacancy. And then in sports you go for the best player

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:28.799
but in the company people suppressing you and say well what

00:17:28.819 --> 00:17:32.730
about Mike, Mike has been here for 20 years, I mean he

00:17:32.750 --> 00:17:35.750
deserves it you know, okay he is not the sharpest tool in

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:39.220
the den but I mean he's pretty good, people like him, I am

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:42.599
sure it's going to be okay and then you have Jane who just

00:17:42.599 --> 00:17:47.200
joined two years ago and deserves that big time because

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:49.529
built a track record, is a culture ambassador, has

00:17:49.549 --> 00:17:53.549
potential, will do it, is much better fit and sometimes if

00:17:53.569 --> 00:17:57.889
you promote Mike, nobody will ask you questions but Jane; if

00:17:57.909 --> 00:18:00.839
you promote Jane, Jane won't ask you a question but

00:18:00.859 --> 00:18:04.399
everybody else will. And guess what, if you promote Mike,

00:18:04.419 --> 00:18:10.099
Jane sees through that and she's leaving and she leaves and

00:18:10.119 --> 00:18:13.119
all of a sudden you are left with only the mediocre people

00:18:13.119 --> 00:18:15.619
who thinks that this thing of patronizing and promoting the

00:18:15.619 --> 00:18:18.369
senior guys is the best thing to do as opposed to

00:18:18.369 --> 00:18:21.680
meritocracy. The worst thing in the company since you cannot

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:24.009
please everybody that's another lesson that I learned time

00:18:24.009 --> 00:18:27.069
and time again, if you cannot please everybody, please the

00:18:27.089 --> 00:18:32.170
most talented ones. I would rather have the most talented

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:35.859
people say visit my company and people that are mediocre say

00:18:35.879 --> 00:18:39.129
oh man this is drag, I think I am going to leave this

00:18:39.129 --> 00:18:47.049
company, you know great, they are going to leave the company

00:18:47.069 --> 00:18:49.769
because sometime the way to solve a problem is to create a

00:18:49.769 --> 00:18:53.129
problem. That's why when you have a poor performing guy, you

00:18:53.149 --> 00:18:56.859
should give 2-3 feedbacks of course because people again

00:18:56.879 --> 00:19:00.369
they go with feedback constructive, positive feedback but

00:19:00.389 --> 00:19:02.799
after 3 feedbacks, a year went past, the guy's not

00:19:02.799 --> 00:19:06.619
performing, it's better for both of you to let that guy go

00:19:06.639 --> 00:19:10.299
first because you create a problem, that vacancy and in

00:19:10.319 --> 00:19:13.319
creating a problem you have to solve the problem. If the guy

00:19:13.319 --> 00:19:15.819
remains there for 10 years because you don't have the

00:19:15.819 --> 00:19:18.649
problem, you don't have to urge to solve the problem. So

00:19:18.649 --> 00:19:21.329
create the problem, let the guy go because that guy could be

00:19:21.329 --> 00:19:24.940
a huge success in another company but not here. So don't

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:27.849
waste his time, life's too short, don't waste his or her

00:19:27.849 --> 00:19:32.349
time. Tell him or her what they need to know and move on,

00:19:32.369 --> 00:19:36.149
okay. So that sounds like harsh but it's not, think about

00:19:36.169 --> 00:19:39.529
sports, that's what happens and we accept it; I am not

00:19:39.549 --> 00:19:45.149
saying we like it but we accept it. Another thing that great

00:19:45.169 --> 00:19:48.720
people lack is informality. Informality is not the way you

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:51.329
address but it's the environment you create in the company

00:19:51.329 --> 00:19:54.240
that people can speak up, speak their minds as long as they

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:57.139
are constructive and respectful. That's why in our office

00:19:57.159 --> 00:20:01.240
for example nobody has an office space. I don't have an

00:20:01.259 --> 00:20:05.680
office space. I am the CEO of the company but I share a big

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:08.440
table with all my direct reports and in all our offices

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:11.230
across the world, everybody has the same kind of layout.

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:16.170
Why? Because that gets information flowing, that gets people

00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:20.259
to connect in two-minute meetings, five-minute meetings as

00:20:20.279 --> 00:20:22.859
they interact across the table or they meet somebody across

00:20:22.879 --> 00:20:26.680
the corridor. Think of that. Not only outlook would allow

00:20:26.700 --> 00:20:33.009
you to schedule 5 minute meetings, right. I mean I haven't

00:20:33.029 --> 00:20:35.529
got that but you are sure to get with people and you

00:20:35.529 --> 00:20:36.529
interact with

00:20:36.529 --> 00:20:38.779
[inaudible] in two minutes, he is talking about Brazil and

00:20:38.779 --> 00:20:41.549
we interact two minutes here and then somebody asks about

00:20:41.569 --> 00:20:44.909
something and you are interacting here 5 minutes, bang you

00:20:44.929 --> 00:20:48.940
have got much stuff done and people are exposed both ways.

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:52.230
So no place to hide, much better for you to get to know

00:20:52.250 --> 00:20:54.700
people who is doing what. So, open environment is something

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:57.700
that great people like, mediocre people would love to be

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:09.299
behind a closed door playing games and stuff.

00:21:09.319 --> 00:21:12.920
So candor, candor is the other thing. I mean talented people

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:16.099
like to know where they stand because they know life's too

00:21:16.119 --> 00:21:18.960
short, they know they have other options even in tough

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:22.509
economies people always have options and you have to tell

00:21:22.529 --> 00:21:25.779
them where they stand. This is going well, this is a gap, we

00:21:25.779 --> 00:21:29.029
do it twice a year and this is the plans, what's your future

00:21:29.029 --> 00:21:31.779
here, what do you think what are the next steps, talented

00:21:31.779 --> 00:21:33.990
people like that. Talented people are high maintenance

00:21:34.009 --> 00:21:37.129
people. It's much easier to work with mediocre good people

00:21:37.149 --> 00:21:40.799
in that respect, they never come to you, they try to hide

00:21:40.819 --> 00:21:45.659
from you, they loved the closed doors. Talented people ask

00:21:45.679 --> 00:21:49.409
whoever what about my future, am I doing okay, I have an

00:21:49.429 --> 00:21:52.549
idea, I don't think this is going well, I think the company

00:21:52.549 --> 00:21:54.549
is missing those opportunities, and it's high maintenance.

00:21:54.549 --> 00:21:58.359
But that's much better to have that than to have people that

00:21:58.379 --> 00:22:01.059
don't want to talk to you and when you talk to them they

00:22:01.059 --> 00:22:04.059
have no ideas, they think that status quo is the best thing

00:22:04.059 --> 00:22:06.559
that can happen, right, which is never the case especially

00:22:06.559 --> 00:22:10.069
in the moments and the kind of world we live today. So again

00:22:10.089 --> 00:22:15.359
just trying to summarize meritocracy, the thing about the

00:22:15.379 --> 00:22:19.049
people that you need to dedicate time with people just like

00:22:19.069 --> 00:22:22.750
a coach in a successful sports team would do it, think about

00:22:22.769 --> 00:22:26.460
that. For 20 years of my life that was the case, why should

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:29.700
I forget all that and start behaving in a weird low

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:33.139
performing type way to build a low performance culture. Is

00:22:33.159 --> 00:22:36.159
that what we are going to have after all these years of

00:22:36.159 --> 00:22:38.909
studying business, no of course not. Again I think it's very

00:22:38.909 --> 00:22:41.909
important for guys like you that are going to be leaders in

00:22:41.909 --> 00:22:44.909
the organization is to know what kind of pressure to put on

00:22:44.909 --> 00:22:49.210
the system. Look at the Olympics, World Cup, I mean the

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:52.079
giants when they are playing their games, the World Series,

00:22:52.099 --> 00:22:56.059
I mean congratulations by the way and I think 50 years, but

00:22:56.079 --> 00:23:02.680
fine yeah. But you look at the professional athletes

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:06.210
playing, they are not having fun when they are about to

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:10.240
play. Think about the 100 meter dash, they film those guys,

00:23:10.259 --> 00:23:12.759
they are truly focused, they are not thinking about anything

00:23:12.759 --> 00:23:16.009
else other then to get from here to here in 9 seconds or

00:23:16.009 --> 00:23:21.539
whatever, okay. So there are truly focused and there is a

00:23:21.559 --> 00:23:24.309
lot of pressure built from the fact that you have 60000

00:23:24.309 --> 00:23:26.990
people watching them, their contracts, their family, their

00:23:27.009 --> 00:23:31.029
wives, whoever. In a company, if you want the best of

00:23:31.049 --> 00:23:35.579
people, as a leader, you have to put pressure all the time.

00:23:35.599 --> 00:23:38.829
If you put too much pressure people panic that's bad, that's

00:23:38.829 --> 00:23:43.809
bad. You should put too little pressure, people have a great

00:23:43.829 --> 00:23:48.039
time. The targets are easy and they don't expect too much. I

00:23:48.059 --> 00:23:52.279
mean if we grow half of what we could, that's fine, I mean

00:23:52.299 --> 00:23:55.049
that's okay, we will pay bonus for that as well and

00:23:55.049 --> 00:23:58.700
shareholders didn't get their value, there was no value

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:01.930
creation but we will pay bonus anyway and that kind of

00:24:01.950 --> 00:24:05.039
thing. No, you won't get the best of people and again the

00:24:05.039 --> 00:24:07.309
great people will leave, they don't like this kind of

00:24:07.309 --> 00:24:11.180
environment. So as a leader you need to know that people all

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:15.859
of us, we are at our best when we are under pressure,

00:24:15.879 --> 00:24:21.009
healthy pressure. Think about school. When you really get

00:24:21.029 --> 00:24:23.930
your mind around things is when you have a deadline, right.

00:24:23.950 --> 00:24:27.109
I mean you have a deadline, you have that work that you need

00:24:27.109 --> 00:24:31.250
to deliver tomorrow. Man, you are as focused as a razorblade

00:24:31.269 --> 00:24:33.700
and you don't think about anything, you cancel everything,

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:36.779
your social agenda goes to hell, your Facebook is off,

00:24:36.799 --> 00:24:42.269
everything is off, you just work on that thing, that's when

00:24:42.289 --> 00:24:44.789
you get the best of you. When you get transferred within the

00:24:44.789 --> 00:24:48.609
company to, we like to do that a lot what we call the crazy

00:24:48.629 --> 00:24:51.629
promotions, we got a guy from sale put the guy in supply

00:24:51.629 --> 00:24:54.379
chain for example because we believe that that's the way to

00:24:54.379 --> 00:24:57.129
build people. And again, the magic word, take people out of

00:24:57.129 --> 00:25:01.079
their comfort zone; whenever we are in a place that's too

00:25:01.099 --> 00:25:06.230
comfortable, it's bad, that's bad. You have to take from

00:25:06.250 --> 00:25:08.750
time to time people from their comfort zone because that's

00:25:08.750 --> 00:25:12.869
when this whole idea of the athlete 100 meter dash, that

00:25:12.889 --> 00:25:15.750
focus, that thing oh my God I have three months to learn

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:18.639
this new job, that's when you really move to next level. If

00:25:18.639 --> 00:25:22.779
they stay 10 years in the same job, I am not saying the same

00:25:22.799 --> 00:25:25.799
company, lot of people stay 20 years 30 years in our company

00:25:25.799 --> 00:25:29.049
but 10 years doing the same thing, the same job in the same

00:25:29.049 --> 00:25:32.299
region, I mean at some point you get stale, I mean you get

00:25:32.299 --> 00:25:35.289
used to landscape, you don't see A from B, black from white

00:25:35.289 --> 00:25:37.970
because everything has been there such a long time. And you

00:25:37.970 --> 00:25:41.329
can only learn this much per day and if you don't learn

00:25:41.349 --> 00:25:44.349
because you are used to it, you are not going to recover

00:25:44.349 --> 00:25:47.349
that going forward so you waste your time. So that's very

00:25:47.369 --> 00:25:50.000
important too, the whole pressure, take people out of the

00:25:50.019 --> 00:25:52.269
comfort zone, that's the way to develop great people.

00:25:52.269 --> 00:25:55.269
The third leg of the stool is culture. So I spoke about

00:25:55.269 --> 00:25:59.730
dream, people, now culture. Culture in our company we call

00:25:59.750 --> 00:26:02.879
it's a culture of ownership. We don't like the word

00:26:02.899 --> 00:26:07.529
professionals, executives we like the words owners. Think

00:26:07.549 --> 00:26:10.549
about this. Owners are the guys that live and die by their

00:26:10.569 --> 00:26:13.559
business because that's all they have. If you have a bakery

00:26:13.559 --> 00:26:16.869
and that's the way you get your livelihood from, that bakery

00:26:16.889 --> 00:26:20.119
has to succeed, failure is not an option and you have to be

00:26:20.119 --> 00:26:22.119
competitive, you have to understand your customers, you

00:26:22.119 --> 00:26:24.369
cannot lose customers to Wal-Mart just because they opened

00:26:24.369 --> 00:26:27.000
across the street, you have to rethink your business but

00:26:27.019 --> 00:26:30.019
that's your business; you are not going to hop on to next

00:26:30.019 --> 00:26:32.699
job, that's your life. So owners, we like owners because of

00:26:32.699 --> 00:26:36.750
that because they are going to live with the consequences of

00:26:36.769 --> 00:26:40.019
their decision. I like to give an example that caused me

00:26:40.039 --> 00:26:42.819
already some trouble but I keep going back to the same

00:26:42.839 --> 00:26:48.429
example, of the rental car, okay. It doesn't happen to me

00:26:48.449 --> 00:26:52.409
but I have heard some people drive a rental car in a

00:26:52.429 --> 00:26:56.039
different way than they drive their own car but it doesn't

00:26:56.059 --> 00:27:00.309
happen to me. Some people like to try things, weird things

00:27:00.329 --> 00:27:10.059
in rental cars and experiments, experiments. Again, I have

00:27:10.079 --> 00:27:12.829
heard that. I have heard even people that say don't be

00:27:12.829 --> 00:27:19.509
gentle it's a rental. See how weird people can be? The world

00:27:19.529 --> 00:27:26.200
out there is weird place. But I mean rental car, why do

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:29.099
these people behave like that with a rental car? I think it'

00:27:29.099 --> 00:27:31.859
s because they are not going to live with the consequences

00:27:31.879 --> 00:27:35.399
of their driving. So they are going to drive, experiment,

00:27:35.419 --> 00:27:38.919
there will be consequences to the car, they will give back

00:27:38.939 --> 00:27:42.179
the car to one of these companies and somebody else will

00:27:42.199 --> 00:27:44.949
live with the consequence next day. Your car, it's going to

00:27:44.949 --> 00:27:47.449
be your car today, tomorrow, next month, next year, next

00:27:47.449 --> 00:27:50.549
five years, you drive in the different way. We want your car

00:27:50.569 --> 00:27:54.429
to be your company not like a rental company. And you have

00:27:54.449 --> 00:27:58.909
lots of stuff that we do in the company from all the way to

00:27:58.929 --> 00:28:01.990
share ownership which is the classic way but more important

00:28:02.009 --> 00:28:04.359
than that is the mindset. We believe that people have

00:28:04.359 --> 00:28:07.599
shares, options, variable compensation which we

00:28:07.619 --> 00:28:10.440
[inaudible] of that, they first need to have the ownership

00:28:10.460 --> 00:28:13.599
mindset. And there are many things in the company that we do

00:28:13.619 --> 00:28:16.859
to foster that environment that this is my machine. If I

00:28:16.879 --> 00:28:21.710
work at a blue plan and I have a - line or a blue house,

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:26.389
this is my part of the world. There are some rules, this is

00:28:26.409 --> 00:28:30.759
the sandbox but within the sandbox, I own it. We would fail

00:28:30.779 --> 00:28:34.049
if people would have to ask at some point, would have failed

00:28:34.069 --> 00:28:37.109
if at some points our key guys have to ask the questions

00:28:37.129 --> 00:28:41.849
what about if I had my own business. This is your business.

00:28:41.869 --> 00:28:44.579
That's the environmental we try to foster. It's a big

00:28:44.579 --> 00:28:48.349
startup, we always say that. We lack that flexibility of a

00:28:48.369 --> 00:28:51.230
startup where people are always trying to do new things

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:53.519
within of course some very basic

00:28:53.539 --> 00:28:55.709
[inaudible] so we don't lose advantage of being a large

00:28:55.709 --> 00:28:56.709
company.

00:28:56.709 --> 00:28:59.480
So the rental car example is one that I like because I think

00:28:59.480 --> 00:29:02.189
it talks to the essence of ownership and we all understand

00:29:02.189 --> 00:29:05.240
that at least some people understand that. I think other

00:29:05.259 --> 00:29:08.059
things about our culture, a lot of people say they are very

00:29:08.059 --> 00:29:10.950
focused on the consumer, we say that too because the

00:29:10.970 --> 00:29:13.899
consumer is the boss, that's right. I mean think about this.

00:29:13.899 --> 00:29:16.740
People don't need to buy beverages. If tomorrow they decide

00:29:16.759 --> 00:29:21.559
just to drink water out of the tap, I would have no business

00:29:21.579 --> 00:29:23.939
and most of our competitors would have no business so it's

00:29:23.939 --> 00:29:28.990
that simple. But we try to take that to the next level. For

00:29:29.009 --> 00:29:31.669
example, we try to take that to the way we manage the

00:29:31.669 --> 00:29:34.659
company. I try to think if the guy guying my

00:29:34.679 --> 00:29:39.589
[inaudible] is paying a premium over my Budweiser, Bud Light

00:29:39.609 --> 00:29:43.629
other products, what do they expect me to do with that money

00:29:43.649 --> 00:29:47.119
as a consumer? If I have lavish offices and corporate jets

00:29:47.139 --> 00:29:51.409
and five course meals when I travel and Four Seasons and all

00:29:51.429 --> 00:29:55.029
that, we don't see the acid test. Would our consumers be

00:29:55.049 --> 00:29:58.889
proud if they knew we spent their money like that or would

00:29:58.909 --> 00:30:02.609
they feel better if we have a lean company where we have

00:30:02.629 --> 00:30:05.389
more money to spend on things that are important for them,

00:30:05.409 --> 00:30:08.789
an NFL sponsorship, a major league ball sponsorship, the

00:30:08.809 --> 00:30:12.409
Olympics, the World Cup so many things that they like that

00:30:12.429 --> 00:30:16.049
they see as a return for what they are paying for the

00:30:16.069 --> 00:30:19.829
product. So we try to be lean not only because it's the

00:30:19.849 --> 00:30:22.389
right thing to do but because our consumers would be proud

00:30:22.389 --> 00:30:26.289
of us knowing that we are managing their money in a way to

00:30:26.309 --> 00:30:29.599
have more to give back as opposed to spend in between

00:30:29.619 --> 00:30:34.000
friends in the company. So we are very lean because we

00:30:34.019 --> 00:30:37.849
believe so we call this the cross connect win. The more non-

00:30:37.869 --> 00:30:41.429
working dollars that are dollars that consumers are not

00:30:41.449 --> 00:30:44.449
willing to pay a premium for because they don't see it, they

00:30:44.449 --> 00:30:47.199
don't touch it, don't drink it, don't take home, don't pay

00:30:47.199 --> 00:30:49.699
for it, so non-working dollars; the more we get non-working

00:30:49.699 --> 00:30:51.699
dollars to become working dollars, things that consumers

00:30:51.699 --> 00:30:56.259
value, the more we connect with them because they see we are

00:30:56.279 --> 00:30:59.329
giving back to them, the more we win more business and more

00:30:59.349 --> 00:31:04.509
consumers, cross connection win. And that's why the way to

00:31:04.529 --> 00:31:08.059
grow companies is always and I am going to say here the

00:31:08.079 --> 00:31:12.659
finance 101, top line and cost efficiencies, do both. But if

00:31:12.679 --> 00:31:16.339
you only do cost, it's a short thing, can only do so much

00:31:16.359 --> 00:31:19.359
and the way to get people excited about cost because it's a

00:31:19.359 --> 00:31:22.109
tough thing to get people excited about is to connect that

00:31:22.109 --> 00:31:25.359
with the top line. So the more money we find here, the more

00:31:25.359 --> 00:31:28.109
we are going to revert back to the marketplace where it

00:31:28.109 --> 00:31:29.990
matters and the more consumers in business we will win,

00:31:29.990 --> 00:31:30.990
okay.

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:33.460
Leadership, that's important thing in our culture. Our

00:31:33.480 --> 00:31:37.039
culture is only passed from person to person by means of

00:31:37.059 --> 00:31:40.059
leadership. Your actions as we all know are important than

00:31:40.079 --> 00:31:43.259
words. What's important about leadership, I will tell you in

00:31:43.279 --> 00:31:46.629
5 minutes what we discovered. First, who is the leader in

00:31:46.649 --> 00:31:50.259
the company? We say anybody who needs a team to get to a

00:31:50.279 --> 00:31:54.059
target. If you can get to a target by yourself in that role,

00:31:54.079 --> 00:31:57.219
you are not being a leader, you don't need to be a leader

00:31:57.219 --> 00:31:59.719
you are not in leadership position because by yourself you

00:31:59.719 --> 00:32:03.049
can get your target. If you need a team to get together to

00:32:03.069 --> 00:32:06.500
the target, you are in the leadership position. What's the

00:32:06.519 --> 00:32:10.549
definition of leadership? I mean people write books about

00:32:10.569 --> 00:32:14.919
it, we have three, three dimensions. Leadership is all about

00:32:14.939 --> 00:32:18.829
delivering results on a consistent basis with your team

00:32:18.849 --> 00:32:21.789
because by their definition, you cannot do it alone, do it

00:32:21.809 --> 00:32:26.029
the right way. So you can be on top and you can replicate

00:32:26.049 --> 00:32:30.909
the way you did it to other parts of the business. That's

00:32:30.929 --> 00:32:35.039
leadership. Leadership's a very important trait in guys like

00:32:35.059 --> 00:32:37.809
you that are going to join companies. Very hard to define

00:32:37.809 --> 00:32:41.449
but you know when you see a good one. These three things are

00:32:41.469 --> 00:32:46.629
just basic definitions but I like to say other things about

00:32:46.649 --> 00:32:49.659
leadership. Leadership is a guy that can see the world out

00:32:49.679 --> 00:32:53.069
there which is a very complex world and translate that by

00:32:53.089 --> 00:32:57.250
analysis into simple things. Think about this. I have a

00:32:57.269 --> 00:33:01.199
100000 plus people in our company, we have 70,000 that are

00:33:01.219 --> 00:33:06.159
sales reps. They are very smart, they are street smart but I

00:33:06.179 --> 00:33:08.960
don't have time to explain them everything that happens in

00:33:08.980 --> 00:33:10.859
the marketplace in terms of deep

00:33:10.879 --> 00:33:13.500
[inaudible] analysis that we do unless this is everything we

00:33:13.519 --> 00:33:16.269
do in the marketplace. Not because they can get it because

00:33:16.269 --> 00:33:19.019
they don't have time, they are busy, they are the ones

00:33:19.019 --> 00:33:21.569
bringing the money in facing the customers. They need to

00:33:21.589 --> 00:33:25.000
have customer facing time. So, as a leader, we need to

00:33:25.019 --> 00:33:27.519
understand the world out there which is very complex but

00:33:27.519 --> 00:33:31.419
translate that into two, three, five action things that can

00:33:31.439 --> 00:33:35.669
be done by 80,000 people. Think about the automobile, think

00:33:35.689 --> 00:33:38.759
about the automobile. How many of us drive here? Everybody.

00:33:38.779 --> 00:33:41.549
Think about if we had to understand about thermodynamics,

00:33:41.569 --> 00:33:46.869
mechanical engineer, I mean everything that goes into a car

00:33:46.889 --> 00:33:51.659
under the hood. Somebody was smart enough to put two pedals

00:33:51.679 --> 00:33:55.299
and a steering wheel and a shift that everybody drives even

00:33:55.319 --> 00:34:00.259
without knowing what's under the hood. That's genius. I mean

00:34:00.279 --> 00:34:03.740
it took a very complex type machine and you made it so

00:34:03.759 --> 00:34:06.659
simple to operate that billions of people around the world

00:34:06.679 --> 00:34:08.889
can operate. That's the thing about leadership in a company.

00:34:08.889 --> 00:34:12.840
90% of the people in the company if you were to compare with

00:34:12.860 --> 00:34:17.489
a ship need to be running the engine room. Well you need

00:34:17.509 --> 00:34:21.110
some people in the bridge looking at the bridge looking at

00:34:21.130 --> 00:34:24.059
where the ship's going but the 90% doesn't need to know

00:34:24.059 --> 00:34:26.589
that, they need to because they have a busy life, very busy

00:34:26.589 --> 00:34:29.900
life. So leadership's about that. Leadership's about not

00:34:29.920 --> 00:34:33.190
being selfish. If you have questions about your company, try

00:34:33.210 --> 00:34:36.989
to get them clear because you reference to your team, okay.

00:34:37.009 --> 00:34:38.819
So those are some things about leadership.

00:34:38.839 --> 00:34:42.389
The other thing, last one in culture, there is no shortcuts.

00:34:42.409 --> 00:34:46.559
We work a lot with Jim Collins that used to be a professor

00:34:46.579 --> 00:34:49.579
here at business school. I met him in '88-'87 when he was

00:34:49.579 --> 00:34:54.469
here and he works a lot with us. Once I taped an interview

00:34:54.489 --> 00:34:57.840
with him for a global conference of ours and I asked Jim, I

00:34:57.860 --> 00:35:01.789
read your book Good to Great. For me, it's so commonsense

00:35:01.809 --> 00:35:04.559
and it's even better because it's based on research so it's

00:35:04.559 --> 00:35:06.809
not your opinion, it's based on research; why would

00:35:06.809 --> 00:35:09.809
companies choose not to do what's in your book if that's a

00:35:09.809 --> 00:35:13.059
recipe book to go from good to great. And he says do you

00:35:13.059 --> 00:35:16.059
know what the problem is in my research in context with the

00:35:16.059 --> 00:35:20.380
companies? All companies start with the same good ideas and

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:23.659
a good intent but at some point they start looking for

00:35:23.679 --> 00:35:27.730
shortcuts. So instead of forming their own people, building

00:35:27.750 --> 00:35:31.349
their own strength, they go to market and hire. Somebody

00:35:31.369 --> 00:35:34.139
else did their job for them, right. Instead of hiring

00:35:34.159 --> 00:35:37.409
somebody out of school, 22 years old and form this guy to be

00:35:37.409 --> 00:35:40.889
a great marketer, why not go to a great marketing company

00:35:40.909 --> 00:35:45.190
out there, at P&amp;G or Coke or Pepsi and hire somebody from

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:48.769
this company. But then you hire people from other companies

00:35:48.789 --> 00:35:51.809
and you don't have the culture, the fabric that holds

00:35:51.829 --> 00:35:56.000
everybody together and that takes 20 years to build, in one

00:35:56.019 --> 00:36:00.440
month, it's gone. So that's why in his view, and I agree, a

00:36:00.460 --> 00:36:03.509
lot of good companies never get to be great because they are

00:36:03.529 --> 00:36:06.119
looking for shortcuts. They don't understand that to build a

00:36:06.119 --> 00:36:10.389
great company, it takes time it's brick by brick, person by

00:36:10.409 --> 00:36:13.659
person. Yes, it takes a long time but once you build it and

00:36:13.659 --> 00:36:16.659
you keep the gap to your competitors, it's hard for them to

00:36:16.659 --> 00:36:19.659
catch up because lot of the things I am talking about here,

00:36:19.659 --> 00:36:21.969
Dream-People-Culture is very invisible,

00:36:21.989 --> 00:36:24.019
[inaudible] this will come as well but it's about what's

00:36:24.019 --> 00:36:27.340
behind that's invisible, that's hard to touch, it takes 10

00:36:27.360 --> 00:36:32.880
years to build. To finish up, let me tell you this. For me,

00:36:32.900 --> 00:36:36.789
that's the best kept secret in our company. I am not saying

00:36:36.809 --> 00:36:40.309
it's the only way to do it, I am just saying it's the best

00:36:40.309 --> 00:36:43.059
kept secret. You know what because every time I talk about

00:36:43.059 --> 00:36:46.069
this not to you but people from other companies, other CEOs

00:36:46.089 --> 00:36:49.440
and stuff, the reaction I get is of course but this is so

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:52.789
commonsense. I mean having a dream, big idea, hiring the

00:36:52.809 --> 00:36:55.730
very best people and then having this culture of ownership

00:36:55.750 --> 00:36:59.489
to your company, no shortcuts and stuff. And for me, it's

00:36:59.509 --> 00:37:02.349
great that they think about that especially my competitors

00:37:02.369 --> 00:37:07.059
because they take it for granted and they don't do what we

00:37:07.079 --> 00:37:10.759
try to do which is not to take it for granted, talk about it

00:37:10.779 --> 00:37:14.110
every single time we have an opportunity to do a town hall,

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:17.139
to do a Q&amp;A with our employees. I always do a 10 minute

00:37:17.139 --> 00:37:20.509
Dream-People-Culture introduction and then to give time to

00:37:20.529 --> 00:37:24.429
warm up and then it is because this is what we are all

00:37:24.449 --> 00:37:27.150
about, this is what brought us here from a very small

00:37:27.150 --> 00:37:29.980
company to the top five consumer goods company. We are very

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:32.719
humbled to say that because we have to earn it everyday,

00:37:32.719 --> 00:37:35.529
there is no such a thing as oh and we got there, no, no, no,

00:37:35.529 --> 00:37:39.130
the more you go the more you have to fall. We know that in a

00:37:39.130 --> 00:37:42.789
very humble way but at least I can show you this is not a

00:37:42.809 --> 00:37:45.989
concept with no proof. The other thing I can tell you is

00:37:46.009 --> 00:37:50.130
that we grew our company organically and also through M&amp;A,

00:37:50.150 --> 00:37:52.980
Anheuser-Busch being the last one we did. But in the last 12

00:37:52.980 --> 00:37:57.119
years we did five cross country's big combinations we call

00:37:57.139 --> 00:38:01.259
it. And every time we combine with a company in all sector,

00:38:01.279 --> 00:38:04.190
we ask the questions because we could see the guts of our

00:38:04.190 --> 00:38:06.989
competitors in different countries and say okay why are we

00:38:07.009 --> 00:38:09.759
performing as a company on average, we are not perfect, far

00:38:09.759 --> 00:38:12.259
from it but on average performing better than this other

00:38:12.259 --> 00:38:15.349
company. And the only answer I could always get to was

00:38:15.369 --> 00:38:18.420
Dream-People-Culture in the sense that we had a group of

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:22.339
people that aimed higher, had a sense of purpose, better

00:38:22.359 --> 00:38:25.929
higher motivated than this other company. I had people that

00:38:25.949 --> 00:38:29.250
were better, more talented and had a culture of ownership.

00:38:29.269 --> 00:38:33.119
This is my company not my job and that made a huge

00:38:33.139 --> 00:38:36.529
difference. So, with that, I would like to open for

00:38:36.549 --> 00:38:59.869
questions. Thank you very much.

00:38:59.889 --> 00:39:00.889
[Audience]

00:39:00.889 --> 00:39:16.789
Very good point, very good point. We do that for example,

00:39:16.809 --> 00:39:20.099
the best example would be for example the merger we did with

00:39:20.119 --> 00:39:23.130
Anheuser-Busch, the Budweiser company just two years ago in

00:39:23.150 --> 00:39:27.719
the midst of this whole financial crisis and stuff. What we

00:39:27.739 --> 00:39:30.929
do, and this was the first one we did, we learned that when

00:39:30.929 --> 00:39:34.179
you do such a thing you have to start talking. When I went

00:39:34.179 --> 00:39:36.929
there to St. Louis the day after we signed the merger

00:39:36.929 --> 00:39:39.429
agreement, I went there, I gathered the top leadership of

00:39:39.429 --> 00:39:43.319
the company. All I spoke about was Dream-People-Culture

00:39:43.339 --> 00:39:46.699
because once you set that framework, every question you go

00:39:46.719 --> 00:39:50.079
back to that framework. So when they said oh what's your

00:39:50.099 --> 00:39:53.349
opinion about this, I said well as I said and we have a

00:39:53.349 --> 00:39:55.599
thing called 10 Principles so Dream-People-Culture for us if

00:39:55.599 --> 00:39:58.849
you go to our website you see it's a 10 principle thing one

00:39:58.849 --> 00:40:01.839
for dream, two for people, seven for culture and it's great,

00:40:01.839 --> 00:40:04.839
it's a great frame for you to anchor things. And what you

00:40:04.839 --> 00:40:08.400
have to do in our experience to change, when you talk about

00:40:08.420 --> 00:40:11.789
culture it's important to say that we believe a lot in

00:40:11.809 --> 00:40:14.599
national cultures, we don't want to change those, that's why

00:40:14.599 --> 00:40:17.420
we travel, that's why life is so interesting when you travel

00:40:17.420 --> 00:40:19.659
because people dress differently, spend their free time in a

00:40:19.659 --> 00:40:22.519
different way, have different family ties, eat differently,

00:40:22.539 --> 00:40:25.380
that's why we travel, different architecture. But the

00:40:25.400 --> 00:40:28.610
company, no, we say it's one company one culture. We have

00:40:28.630 --> 00:40:33.000
zero complacency for differences in basic stuff like a

00:40:33.019 --> 00:40:35.809
constitution of a country. So this company that's going to

00:40:35.829 --> 00:40:39.969
join us this is how we operate. And I never met anybody who

00:40:39.989 --> 00:40:42.989
said I don't agree with this. If you want to be high

00:40:42.989 --> 00:40:45.739
performing just like an athlete that's what you have to do,

00:40:45.739 --> 00:40:47.989
have a dream, surround yourself with great people, work

00:40:47.989 --> 00:40:51.739
hard, that's what you have to do. But I am not up for it; I

00:40:51.739 --> 00:40:55.239
have met some people that said that. I am in a stage of my

00:40:55.239 --> 00:40:58.989
life and this and that, I am not up for it but I think if

00:40:58.989 --> 00:41:02.739
you want to do, I agree with this. So what we do is we try

00:41:02.739 --> 00:41:05.489
to get the leadership of the company because the company is

00:41:05.489 --> 00:41:08.489
all about this; you have 20% who are the leaders, 70% who

00:41:08.489 --> 00:41:10.989
will follow mostly with the leaders because they use the

00:41:10.989 --> 00:41:15.079
leaders as their place for Q&amp;A and inspiration and all that

00:41:15.099 --> 00:41:18.610
if the leaders are good leaders and you have at the bottom

00:41:18.630 --> 00:41:21.500
10% or 15% whatever the balance that are always against

00:41:21.519 --> 00:41:26.670
anything. So those guys you have to identify and bang out. I

00:41:26.690 --> 00:41:31.079
think what was good in this last transaction that we did is

00:41:31.099 --> 00:41:34.219
that the first layer of management left day one for

00:41:34.239 --> 00:41:37.710
different reasons. Some because they were retiring, some

00:41:37.730 --> 00:41:40.819
because they were too rich to care because of the price we

00:41:40.839 --> 00:41:45.659
paid and some because we felt wouldn't have a good fit. It

00:41:45.679 --> 00:41:49.190
was great because then we promoted a lot of new people that

00:41:49.210 --> 00:41:52.049
had been waiting for that opportunity and these new people

00:41:52.069 --> 00:41:55.119
said this is my company now and I like this stuff because

00:41:55.139 --> 00:41:57.750
it's all about high performance, meritocracy, openness,

00:41:57.769 --> 00:42:02.059
candor, that's my company, I have been waiting for it so now

00:42:02.079 --> 00:42:04.829
let's do this according to this. So it's all about making

00:42:04.829 --> 00:42:07.329
sure you have the right leaders because the right leaders

00:42:07.329 --> 00:42:10.980
will get that other 60% or 70% of the people along with them

00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:14.599
and they will also identify the bad apples and take them out

00:42:14.619 --> 00:42:20.259
and there is bottom 10%-15% that for sure will have to go

00:42:20.279 --> 00:42:22.029
out, yeah. But it's about the leadership.

00:42:22.029 --> 00:42:23.029
[Audience]

00:42:23.029 --> 00:42:53.029
You are right. When we started expanding as a company, we

00:42:57.029 --> 00:43:01.739
had that thing in mind. And the one I was talking to you

00:43:01.759 --> 00:43:04.739
about national cultures, we said okay are we going to be

00:43:04.759 --> 00:43:07.099
able to find people in all those other different cultures

00:43:07.099 --> 00:43:10.460
that we are going to go, the four national cultures that we

00:43:10.480 --> 00:43:13.579
brought up in a different way that will get excited about

00:43:13.599 --> 00:43:16.599
the way we are trying to build our company or not. And the

00:43:16.599 --> 00:43:19.849
fact of the matter yes, you can find. You find a lot of

00:43:19.849 --> 00:43:22.549
people in those places that had been looking for this kind

00:43:22.549 --> 00:43:25.099
of thing because they came from sports, they came from a

00:43:25.099 --> 00:43:28.449
competitive school and they know that that's what build a

00:43:28.469 --> 00:43:31.279
great performance and they are just waiting for an

00:43:31.299 --> 00:43:33.549
opportunity to go a corporation where those things are

00:43:33.549 --> 00:43:37.099
accepted allowed. But you are right, you have to look harder

00:43:37.119 --> 00:43:40.690
in places like some of the ones with nation but over there

00:43:40.710 --> 00:43:44.299
you find great people that are looking for that kind of

00:43:44.319 --> 00:43:49.539
place where they can be themselves so it is possible.

00:43:49.559 --> 00:43:56.319
Audience: So my question is on delegation and you may be

00:43:56.339 --> 00:43:58.839
using your sports analogy, great coaches know when to direct

00:43:58.839 --> 00:44:01.000
their team and when to just trust that their team will

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:03.719
figure it out. And how do you kind of translate that into

00:44:03.719 --> 00:44:06.789
the business as being a leader when to delegate and when to

00:44:06.809 --> 00:44:08.279
make the decision yourself?

00:44:08.299 --> 00:44:11.529
I think that's a very good question. I think the people that

00:44:11.529 --> 00:44:14.569
we value in our business, it's a very different mix. As you

00:44:14.569 --> 00:44:19.309
go up your organization, you have to keep apparently that

00:44:19.329 --> 00:44:23.989
false dilemma that you have to think more where the company

00:44:24.009 --> 00:44:27.369
is going strategically but you cannot lose touch with the

00:44:27.389 --> 00:44:29.569
business. That's why we try to have fewer layers in the

00:44:29.569 --> 00:44:34.269
company, open office space. We value leaders that can ask

00:44:34.289 --> 00:44:37.579
questions that are very detailed but not losing sight of the

00:44:37.599 --> 00:44:41.579
big picture. That's what we value. Because people when they

00:44:41.599 --> 00:44:45.039
are having meetings with me they know, I am not saying I am

00:44:45.059 --> 00:44:48.309
the best but I mean they know because I have been in the

00:44:48.309 --> 00:44:51.059
company for a long time that I can ask questions specially

00:44:51.059 --> 00:44:54.650
sales because that's my background detailed questions. I

00:44:54.670 --> 00:44:58.489
always say that when I go to a meeting, a board meeting, 80%

00:44:58.509 --> 00:45:02.150
of the board meeting is my preparation. The benefit that I

00:45:02.170 --> 00:45:05.309
have to go to a board meeting, 80% is myself getting

00:45:05.329 --> 00:45:08.670
prepared for that conversation, I have a tough board, 20% I

00:45:08.690 --> 00:45:11.710
learn with the board. I think the meetings I have with my

00:45:11.730 --> 00:45:15.630
people is the same thing, 80% is them preparing with their

00:45:15.650 --> 00:45:18.889
people to try to understand the drivers, the root causes of

00:45:18.909 --> 00:45:22.329
the issues and come prepared, 20% will come in our

00:45:22.349 --> 00:45:27.199
discussion but 80% is the preparation. But this capacity to

00:45:27.219 --> 00:45:30.880
not lose sight of the big picture but too comfortable in

00:45:30.900 --> 00:45:34.829
selecting one or two areas and there you go deep that's

00:45:34.849 --> 00:45:37.409
appreciated by your organization because they say I can

00:45:37.429 --> 00:45:40.980
count on him to help us here and that also gets your

00:45:41.000 --> 00:45:44.009
meetings to be much more productive because people prepare

00:45:44.029 --> 00:45:46.179
because they know at some point you can ask here or there,

00:45:46.179 --> 00:45:48.989
they never know, okay. You cannot micromanage the company,

00:45:49.009 --> 00:45:52.239
that's bad. If you micromanage the company, you create a

00:45:52.259 --> 00:45:54.869
whole bunch of people like sometimes, sometimes in the army

00:45:54.889 --> 00:45:58.920
that I used to follow orders. We want people to think but

00:45:58.940 --> 00:46:01.210
they should know that despite your thinking more

00:46:01.230 --> 00:46:04.119
strategically most of the times that you can go deep, that's

00:46:04.119 --> 00:46:07.250
why we go a lot to the market, we visit the market, we hear

00:46:07.250 --> 00:46:09.750
from customers, we like to be with our frontline people

00:46:09.750 --> 00:46:11.750
because that's where you learn what's going on.

00:46:11.750 --> 00:46:18.710
[Audience] Two questions real quick. How do you continue to

00:46:18.730 --> 00:46:22.049
push yourself out of your comfort zone, and two, what's your

00:46:22.069 --> 00:46:23.069
favorite beer?

00:46:23.069 --> 00:46:33.750
My favorite beer is Budweiser, the king of beers. For 20

00:46:33.769 --> 00:46:40.670
years we had this dream of doing something with them with

00:46:40.690 --> 00:46:43.920
Anheuser-Busch, never worked, and it finally worked after 20

00:46:43.940 --> 00:46:48.480
years so that's something that we always had in our minds as

00:46:48.500 --> 00:46:51.250
the beer that can be a global beer, an iconic brand,

00:46:51.250 --> 00:46:54.799
American iconic brand so much so as Starbucks, Coca Cola,

00:46:54.819 --> 00:46:58.619
Pepsi so many other iconic brands that sell so well around

00:46:58.639 --> 00:47:02.539
the world. America continues to be an inspirational place; I

00:47:02.559 --> 00:47:05.319
am not American I am Brazilian but I recognize that through

00:47:05.339 --> 00:47:09.650
research, America continues to be a magnet for young people

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:12.480
around the world, talented people, young people, people that

00:47:12.500 --> 00:47:15.420
want to make things happen and have an opportunity, they all

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:18.420
want to come here. America is taken for granted but people

00:47:18.440 --> 00:47:22.750
from outside value and know that the opportunities are here.

00:47:22.769 --> 00:47:24.819
To your first question, what was it again?

00:47:24.839 --> 00:47:29.690
[Audience] How do you continue to push yourself out of your

00:47:29.710 --> 00:47:30.710
comfort zone?

00:47:30.710 --> 00:47:37.389
Two things from underneath and from above. First, I am very

00:47:37.409 --> 00:47:40.849
critical of myself, have always been so people say when I am

00:47:40.869 --> 00:47:44.199
critical to other people and tough on other people they say

00:47:44.219 --> 00:47:46.929
Brito we accept that because we know you do even worse with

00:47:46.929 --> 00:47:50.380
yourself. So I am the kind of guy that for me to celebrate

00:47:50.400 --> 00:47:52.900
anything is very hard, that's the criticism I often get

00:47:52.900 --> 00:47:55.900
because I am always looking for next step and always it was

00:47:55.900 --> 00:48:00.089
good but this, and everybody says that what's important in a

00:48:00.109 --> 00:48:03.750
sentence is what comes after the but. You should never

00:48:03.769 --> 00:48:06.819
listen before the but. When somebody says oh yeah it was all

00:48:06.839 --> 00:48:09.359
great and stuff but, then you listen because that's what

00:48:09.359 --> 00:48:14.519
matters. So I push myself out of my comfort zone because of

00:48:14.539 --> 00:48:17.710
the board I have, as I said in the other question, a very

00:48:17.710 --> 00:48:20.829
tough board that forces me to up my game all the time,

00:48:20.849 --> 00:48:23.829
because I hire people that are better than I am and because

00:48:23.829 --> 00:48:27.859
I don't want them to get my job anytime soon, I have to keep

00:48:27.879 --> 00:48:32.359
running and revamping myself in some respects and am very

00:48:32.379 --> 00:48:35.909
critical of myself as well. So I think that's the beauty of

00:48:35.929 --> 00:48:38.309
having great people surrounding you, they give you the

00:48:38.329 --> 00:48:42.500
energy and the motivation to continue to revamp, learn, push

00:48:42.519 --> 00:48:46.029
yourself to next level. Again, if you have a whole bunch of

00:48:46.049 --> 00:48:48.549
mediocre people they are going to feel about yourself when

00:48:48.549 --> 00:48:51.309
you shouldn't and then you are going to have the world will

00:48:51.309 --> 00:48:58.059
continue to do this and you are going to be here.

00:48:58.079 --> 00:49:00.579
[Audience] You sound very ambitious and I like that. I have

00:49:00.579 --> 00:49:05.779
two questions, the first question is what's your next merger

00:49:05.799 --> 00:49:11.460
target? The second question is do you have any ambitious

00:49:11.480 --> 00:49:21.440
plans to upgrade the taste of Budweiser?

00:49:21.460 --> 00:49:27.339
Thanks for the question. You know what Frank we can show

00:49:27.359 --> 00:49:32.980
because it's third party research, Budweiser in blind tests,

00:49:33.000 --> 00:49:36.389
blind tests and that makes a huge difference I reckon, in

00:49:36.409 --> 00:49:41.809
blind tests is the best liquid in the domestic market, said

00:49:41.829 --> 00:49:47.670
by consumers. So our challenge is why is that that when we

00:49:47.690 --> 00:49:50.759
label the product people have different perceptions about

00:49:50.779 --> 00:49:55.759
the liquid. We know that we have a challenge there as with

00:49:55.779 --> 00:49:58.759
most iconic brands sometimes you fall in that trap that the

00:49:58.779 --> 00:50:02.119
brand is so iconic that you cannot change it, consumers

00:50:02.139 --> 00:50:05.769
move, the brands stay and now consumers are here, the brand'

00:50:05.789 --> 00:50:10.190
s here. And because of that we defined it as a problem two

00:50:10.210 --> 00:50:14.069
years ago when we merged because before it was not seen as a

00:50:14.089 --> 00:50:17.719
problem because Bud Light was skyrocketing and compensating

00:50:17.739 --> 00:50:22.170
for the Budweiser slippage but we said no, no, these two

00:50:22.190 --> 00:50:25.690
brands are now two huge brands number 1 and 2 in the US and

00:50:25.690 --> 00:50:28.750
in the world so we need to give Budweiser what it deserves.

00:50:28.769 --> 00:50:32.039
We did lot of research, there are a lot of positives, some

00:50:32.059 --> 00:50:35.420
negatives and some other things that we need to bring to the

00:50:35.440 --> 00:50:39.710
brand because of its iconic status. So I am very excited.

00:50:39.730 --> 00:50:42.989
And if you look at Budweiser outside of the US it's growing

00:50:43.009 --> 00:50:47.659
like crazy especially China. Your other question is about

00:50:47.679 --> 00:50:51.309
ambition, I think whenever people ask me in the company

00:50:51.329 --> 00:50:56.219
what's our next step I always say de-leveraging because I

00:50:56.239 --> 00:50:59.659
mean we took a lot of money to be able to do this

00:50:59.679 --> 00:51:04.829
transaction with AB so we are ahead of our de-leverage plan

00:51:04.849 --> 00:51:08.920
and we are very happy with it, our cash flow generation, you

00:51:08.940 --> 00:51:15.039
are right, it's huge, it's north of free cash of 7-8 billion

00:51:15.059 --> 00:51:21.859
a year and that's a real problem, I mean what to do with it.

00:51:21.879 --> 00:51:25.379
But it's a good problem to have and I will think about it

00:51:25.389 --> 00:51:28.639
when we have to cross the bridge. But for now we are using

00:51:28.639 --> 00:51:31.639
that cash flow to pay down debt and pay interest to regain

00:51:31.639 --> 00:51:35.809
our freedom. So again, guys thank you very much for being

00:51:35.829 --> 00:51:37.789
here and thanks for your time. Thank you.

00:51:37.789 --> 00:51:38.789
[Applause]

